@if (Visible)
{
    <div class="modal fade show d-block" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" @onclick="OnClose">
        <div class="modal-dialog modal-lg modal-dialog-scrollable" role="document" @onclick:stopPropagation>
            <div class="modal-content">
                <div class="modal-header">
                    <h5 class="modal-title">How the Deck Analyzer works</h5>
                    <button type="button" class="btn-close" @onclick="OnClose"></button>
                </div>
                <div class="modal-body">

                    <p class="text-muted">
                        The Deck Analyzer evaluates an existing deck, shows its functional role coverage,
                        estimates its power bracket, and surfaces targeted upgrade suggestions and combo interactions.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Choose your commander(s)</h6>
                    <p>
                        Pick your commander(s) using the commander search. If your decklist export includes the
                        commander in the maindeck, the tool filters them out automatically.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">1. Paste and resolve</h6>
                    <p>
                        Paste your decklist in any common export format — plain <code>1 Card Name</code>, Arena
                        (<code>1 Card Name (SET) 123</code>), Moxfield, Archidekt, or MTGO. Section headers,
                        sideboard lines, and collector numbers are stripped automatically. Use <strong>Validate
                        Cards</strong> after pasting to check all names against Scryfall before running the
                        full analysis — unrecognized names are highlighted so you can correct them first.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">2. Card classification <span class="badge bg-secondary fw-normal ms-1" style="font-size: 0.7em;">AI step</span></h6>
                    <p>
                        Every card in your deck is assigned a primary role (Ramp, Card Advantage, Tutor,
                        Disruption, etc.) and optional secondary roles. Each role assignment also carries a
                        fractional weight — a card that primarily ramps but also draws might count as 1.0
                        toward Ramp and 0.5 toward Card Advantage. These weights are why the Coverage Report
                        shows decimal values rather than whole numbers. The cheapest model for your chosen
                        provider handles this — classification is a straightforward task that does not require
                        advanced reasoning, and the cheapest model is more than capable of it.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">3. Coverage Report</h6>
                    <p>
                        Shows how many cards you have in each functional role compared to a balanced-deck
                        baseline. Because cards carry fractional weights across multiple roles, the totals show
                        decimal values and can legitimately exceed 99. <strong>Role Gaps</strong> lists the
                        roles most under-served in your current list, ordered by shortfall, and feeds directly
                        into Upgrade Paths.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">4. Bracket Estimate</h6>
                    <p>
                        The bracket score (1–5) is provided by Commander Spellbook, based on tutor density
                        and the number of combos your deck enables. It is an automated estimate, not a manual
                        power-level review — treat it as a rough signal rather than a definitive ruling.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">5. Upgrade Paths <span class="badge bg-secondary fw-normal ms-1" style="font-size: 0.7em;">AI step</span></h6>
                    <p>
                        Fill in what isn't working and an optional price cap, then click <strong>Get Upgrade
                        Suggestions</strong>. The pipeline runs in two passes:
                    </p>
                    <ol class="mb-2">
                        <li>The cheapest model re-prioritises the role gaps based on your feedback text, so the
                            most impactful gaps are addressed first.</li>
                        <li>Your chosen model proposes specific <strong>add + cut</strong> pairs for each gap —
                            a card to add and a card from your current 99 to cut in its place, with written
                            rationale for both.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p>
                        All suggested additions are validated against the EDHREC recommendation pool for your
                        commander — no hallucinated card names will appear in the results.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">6. Combo Finder</h6>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Queries Commander Spellbook for combos your deck already enables, split into two groups:
                        <strong>complete combos</strong> (all named pieces are in your 99) and
                        <strong>near-misses</strong> (one named piece away from a complete combo). Near-misses
                        are useful for spotting combos worth completing with a single swap.
                    </p>

                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-backdrop fade show"></div>
}
